<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Epirus</h1><p class="st-meta">470 BCE – 232 BCE (approx.) · capital Ambracia</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Kingdom of the Molossians in north-west Greece, whose Aeacid kings led the Epirote peoples and produced Pyrrhus, Rome's most famous Greek opponent.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Molossian kings claimed descent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neoptolemus">Neoptolemus</a>, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achilles">Achilles</a>, and were called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeacidae">Aeacids</a>. Their kingdom in the mountains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> emerges into history in the 5th century BCE; King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tharyps">Tharyps</a>, educated at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/athens">Athens</a>, introduced Greek institutions and letters. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molossians">Molossians</a> gradually led the other Epirote tribes, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaonians">Chaonians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thesprotians">Thesprotians</a>, in an alliance.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Kings ruled alongside an assembly and annual magistrates, swearing to govern by law. The economy rested on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pastoralism">pastoralism</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dodona">Dodona's</a> oracle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> as the great religious centre. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I</a>, brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olympias">Olympias</a> and uncle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>, campaigned in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> until his death in 331 BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrrhus-of-epirus">Pyrrhus</a> (297-272 BCE) made <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ambracia">Ambracia</a> his capital and won costly victories over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrrhus-of-epirus">Pyrrhus</a> died at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/argos">Argos</a> in 272 BCE, his successors struggled against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aetolians">Aetolians</a>. The last of the royal line, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deidamia-ii-of-epirus">Deidamia</a>, was killed around 232 BCE and the monarchy was replaced by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/epirote-league">Epirote League</a>. Pyrrhus survives in the phrase 'Pyrrhic victory'.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-epirus.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Epirus" width="320" height="305"><figcaption>Image: Heinrich Kiepert · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Epirus_antiquus_tabula.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>470 BCE – 232 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ambracia</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Tharyps, Alexander I of Epirus, Pyrrhus</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ancient Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ancient Greek (North-West Greek)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Greece, Albania</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Molossian Kingdom, Aeacid Epirus</dd></dl></aside></div>
